{
    "title": "Technical Amendments to Commission Forms",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 11, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-11/pdf/2025-22583.pdf",
    "summary": "Today\u2019s Federal Register includes three quick but important updates: the Federal Aviation Administration is updating several published flight procedures, the Securities and Exchange Commission is fixing the mailing address on a handful of forms, and the Drug Enforcement Administration is extending strict controls on a synthetic drug for another year.\n\nFor aviation, the agency is making immediate changes to certain takeoff and landing procedures used in low visibility at airports in North Carolina, New Jersey, and Kansas. These updates were first issued as emergency notices for safety and are now being formally adopted. The goal is straightforward: reflect current conditions at specific airports and reduce risk. Pilots, air traffic controllers, and passengers benefit from clearer, safer procedures. On the flip side, airlines, corporate flight departments, and general aviation pilots may need to update charts, avionics databases, and training on short notice, which can be disruptive even if the overall economic impact is expected to be small.\n\nOn the markets side, the securities regulator is correcting the Washington, DC office address on several forms. This is purely a housekeeping fix to ensure filings reference the correct location at 100 F Street NE. Filers and compliance teams benefit from reduced confusion and fewer bounced mailings. The likely downside is minimal\u2014primarily the need for firms and vendors to refresh templates and internal references.\n\nFor public health and safety, the drug agency is extending the temporary placement of MDMB-4en-PINACA, a synthetic cannabinoid, in the most restricted category under federal law for up to one more year or until a final decision is made. This continues the tight controls, penalties, and oversight that apply to substances with no accepted medical use and a high potential for harm. Communities and law enforcement may see this as a protective step. However, researchers and legitimate labs could face added hurdles, licensing needs, and delays in studying the substance or related compounds, which can slow scientific progress.\n\nIn short, these actions aim to keep systems current and safe: updated flight procedures now, cleaner paperwork for market participants, and continued restrictions on a risky drug while a permanent decision is pending.",
